WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.909
Ah, ye olde mission-matching and CLIR-matching, a time-honored

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.780
tradition in causing wrong answers.

3
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:06.340
So

4
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.060
you might be wondering, "Hey, I really do want to just

5
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.040
go into the answer choices and be like, 'Is that my mission? Is that my CLIR?

6
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:15.880
Is that my mission?'"

7
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.840
People can mix and match which one they want to match,

8
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.020
whether they're trying to match the CLIR in the answers or trying to match the

9
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.020
mission in the answers. And I think probably some of the language I have in the

10
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.000
Loophole is guilty of feeding this

11
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.400
desire of just going in the answers and not really asking, "Does this

12
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.080
describe what the part of the argument is doing?" Or, "Does this weaken the

13
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.580
conclusion?" Or whatever

14
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.040
backup plan it might be, but instead saying, "Is this my mission?

15
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.160
Is this my CLIR?"

16
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.560
I'm probably guilty of feeding that in certain ways. But I am here to correct it.

17
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.960
Going into the answer choices with the sole desire to just match

18
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.960
your mission or match your CLIR is not a high-accuracy

19
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.860
technique. And it's kind of one of those 80% solutions like in the

20
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.200
other video about early answer lock-in, where you aren't punished all the time,

21
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.740
especially if you're quite good at CLIRing and quite good at making missions.

22
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.490
There are going to be times, of course, where it does match, and that feels really

23
00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.960
good, and it is correct, and everybody's happy.

24
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.750
But you actually don't need to enter the answer choices

25
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.340
with the goal of mission-matching or CLIR-matching in order to

26
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.820
detect a CLIR match or a mission match.

27
00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.320
I liken it to

28
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.940
if I went to an outdoor café right now, and I'm sitting and I'm getting my

29
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.740
table or whatever, I'm set up, and I look, and I see a friend from

30
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.700
college, and I recognize them, and I'm like, "Connor, it's

31
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.360
you, ha!" And I go up to them, everything's great.

32
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.820
I noticed Connor not because I was scanning every table at this

33
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.720
café for friends from high school.

34
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.060
Not high school, from college. I noticed Connor

35
00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:56.160
because I know what Connor looks like, and it's subconsciously in the back of

36
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.720
my brain, and it immediately gets activated when I recognize it.

37
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.520
And so just by being present in the moment, I'm going to know it when I see it.

38
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:07.560
I don't have to make it my conscious goal to go find friends from

39
00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.600
college in order to recognize one.

40
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.620
And here, you just made this mission or CLIR less than a minute ago,

41
00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.470
whereas I have not seen Connor in a long time.

42
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.520
And so your subconscious mind is capable of a lot more than you would think,

43
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.540
that you very likely will recognize the mission or recognize the CLIR if you do

44
00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.360
see it in the answers without making that your entire

45
00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.200
answer choice process. And so why is it bad, though?

46
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.760
Why am I even cautioning you against this?

47
00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.120
Well, it's bad because it very often leads to keywording.

48
00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.340
Very often, people will not actually find the thing that

49
00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:45.260
really matches their CLIR. They'll find something that's expressed in a similar

50
00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.070
way, that has similar words to their CLIR or their mission, and they'll pick that

51
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.180
just because it sounds similar even though it doesn't actually mean the same thing.

52
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.060
And it's not as high accuracy a technique as using the backup

53
00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:01.079
plan. The backup plan is a perfectly suited metric for every single answer

54
00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:05.020
choice and will always work, whereas no matter how good you are

55
00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.609
at CLIRing, how good you are at making missions, there are going to be times where

56
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.810
you don't want it. They just went a different direction in the answer choices.

57
00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.760
And that's totally fine. We do the CLIR and do the mission for different reasons,

58
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.700
and I'll get to those in a second. But you want to get those

59
00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.780
questions right, too, and you want to get the questions right where they go in a

60
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.540
different direction without having to reset the answers just because you were

61
00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.780
trying to use a cheap metric to pick.

62
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.900
And so if you allow the mission or CLIR-matching sense to just be a

63
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.420
back-of-the-mind sort of subconscious thing that, "I'll recognize it when I see it.

64
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:40.180
I don't have to actively hunt for the mission or the CLIR,"

65
00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.480
then you're going to be well-served just going through the backup plan and allowing

66
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.480
the mission and the CLIR to appear to you.

67
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.710
And so then you might say, "Well, Ellen, if I'm not trying to match the mission or

68
00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.380
the CLIR in the answer choices, what's the frigging point of doing this mission and

69
00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.840
this CLIR? I should just save time and screw it."

70
00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.660
Well, that matching game is actually not why we do the CLIR or why

71
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.520
we do the mission. We do the CLIR to force ourselves to develop an analytical

72
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.480
relationship with the stimulus.

73
00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.340
In all my years of teaching the LSAT before the Loophole came out, the thing that I

74
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.060
really noticed was if there wasn't a deliverable at the end of the

75
00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.760
stimulus, like a thing you had to do that required analytical

76
00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:19.601
engagement, I never got analytical engagement.

77
00:04:19.851 --> 00:04:23.760
And so the CLIR is there as a goal for you to hit so that you have a virtuous

78
00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.700
relationship with the stimulus throughout, so that you have a good stimulus process

79
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.780
and an automatic analytical output.

80
00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:33.660
You get so good with the stimulus by clearing it every single time

81
00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.980
without changing it via question type.

82
00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.680
Yes, the CLIR is not affected by question type. Just putting that out there.

83
00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.000
You want it to be an automatic response to a certain kind of stimulus.

84
00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.220
And then for the mission, we're doing our mission to show that we actually

85
00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.360
know the answer to the question ourselves.

86
00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.460
My thought is if I can't do it myself, I have no business judging anyone else doing

87
00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.080
it. And so if I don't know the answer to this question, which

88
00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:59.160
essentially is the mission in most circumstances except weaken and strengthen,

89
00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.400
then I shouldn't be going into the answer choices and being like, "Yeah, you're

90
00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:04.580
wrong. You're wrong. You're wrong." And it's like, girl,

91
00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:06.800
I didn't even know the answer to begin with.

92
00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:09.220
How am I coming in and being like, "Oh, B's an idiot?" I

93
00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.400
didn't know it at all. So in that mission stage, you prove you actually can

94
00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.940
answer the question yourself. You actually do know what's going on.

95
00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.880
And then you tuck it in the back of your mind. If it comes up, great.

96
00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.240
If it doesn't, we use the backup plan, fine.

97
00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:26.400
And so you're ensuring that at every step of the process, you

98
00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.070
are fully covered, fully analytical, fully engaged so that

99
00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:33.520
we end up with lots of right answers and very few wrong ones.

100
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.140
And this actually ends up a lot faster than CLIR and mission-matching.

101
00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:41.440
I know the objection I often hear is it's so much easier to just turn your brain

102
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.160
off and not have to understand the answers to only match.

103
00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:49.120
And I would say that's not really the point of what the LSAT is trying to test.

104
00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:52.910
The LSAT wants you to deeply engage with those answer choices and

105
00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:56.160
improve your translation skills at speed to the point where you really can

106
00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.590
understand those answers. That's why there are so many correct answers that don't

107
00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:03.440
use words from the stimulus or go a different direction than what would be

108
00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.320
anticipated is because they really do want to make sure that you actually are

109
00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.160
understanding those answers fully before you choose.

110
00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:10.480
And so

111
00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:14.290
stop CLIR-matching, stop mission-matching, and

